{"text": " Saving recipes feels like learning to cook. It isn't. Here's why. It's Tuesday, 7 in the evening, and you're tired. You open your 200 saved recipes, scroll for 20 minutes, and end up making toast. Do that four nights a week, and that's 80 minutes just deciding. And you're still no better at cooking than you were in January. So here's the fix. Stop collecting. Pick three dinners you'd happily eat every week and master just those. Say dal, a tomato, pasta, and a bean chili. Cook each one twice this week. By the second time, you'll barely look at the recipe. And write one shopping list for all three. They share the same base, onions, tomatoes, and garlic so nothing goes to waste. So what does your week look like by Sunday? Three recipes, six home dinners, and zero minutes standing at the fridge wondering what to make. Here's the rule. Pick three. Cook each one twice. One list. Send this to the friend with 300 saved recipes. That's what Demure is for. You archive your dream today so you can see it tomorrow and break it down into small steps you can actually do. And follow because this afternoon's real is how 20 minutes a day turned into a wedding gift. If you want to try it out, I've put the link in the bio.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.18, "text": " Saving recipes feels like learning to cook. It isn't.", "tokens": [50363, 34689, 14296, 5300, 588, 4673, 284, 4255, 13, 632, 2125, 470, 13, 50523], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1051505623441754, "compression_ratio": 1.6026936026936027, "no_speech_prob": 0.00197318266145885, "words": [{"word": " Saving", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.5, "probability": 0.8854483366012573}, {"word": " recipes", "start": 0.5, "end": 0.96, "probability": 0.9960393905639648}, {"word": " feels", "start": 0.96, "end": 1.28, "probability": 0.9912689924240112}, {"word": " like", "start": 1.28, "end": 1.56, "probability": 0.9992583394050598}, {"word": " learning", "start": 1.56, "end": 1.82, "probability": 0.